parsyl — error formatting
==========================

Diagnostics for failed parses. Lives alongside `parsyl/parsyl.lsysl`
under the same `module parsyl`.

  - `pos_of(inp)` — `(line, col)` for the cursor position.
  - `format_failure(msg, at)` — multi-line diagnostic with line/col,
    the source line, and a caret under the offending position.

Both walk the source by byte; line/col counting is 1-indexed and
treats `\n` as the line separator (CR-only or CRLF Mac/Windows
sources are scanned correctly because `\n` is the trailing byte in
both cases that matters for line breaks).

    module parsyl

    import std.builder.{new_builder}


pos_of
------

`pos_of(inp)` returns a `(line, col)` pair (both 1-indexed) for the
current cursor position. Walks the source from offset 0 counting
newlines.

    pos_of(inp: Input) -> (int, int) =
        var line = 1
        var col = 1
        var i = 0
        while i < inp.offset do
            if inp.source[i] == 0x0Au8 then
                line = line + 1
                col = 1
            else
                col = col + 1
            i = i + 1
        (line, col)


format_failure
--------------

`format_failure(msg, at)` renders a failure into a multi-line
diagnostic with line number, column, the source line, and a caret
under the offending position. Suitable for direct printing to stderr.

    format_failure(msg: string, at: Input) -> string =
        val (line, col) = pos_of(at)
        var line_start = at.offset
        while line_start > 0 && at.source[line_start - 1] != 0x0Au8 do
            line_start = line_start - 1
        var line_end = at.offset
        while line_end < len(at.source) && at.source[line_end] != 0x0Au8 do
            line_end = line_end + 1
        val src_line = string(at.source[line_start : line_end])
        val b = new_builder()
        b.write(s"line ${line}, col ${col}: ${msg}")
        b.write_byte(0x0Au8)
        b.write(src_line)
        b.write_byte(0x0Au8)
        for i in 0..<col - 1 do
            b.write_byte(0x20u8)
        b.write_byte(0x5Eu8)              // '^'
        b.to_str()
